Texture parking holds a working set that comes back to the memory it needs (Minato, patch 0020).

Why: an app whose parked working set comes back restores many textures within one frame. Each restore's storage and
its 8 MiB staging buffer reach the footprint only as their command buffer runs, so the memory available that parking
reads lags, and before the restores see the emergency band they have kept their packed copies and queued hundreds of
MiB of staging (751 MiB measured). While parking is needed (below 1.5 GiB available) a restore now finishes the
command buffer first once more than 64 MiB of staging would be on its way, and the first restore in the emergency
band lets go the packed copies the restores before it kept. A frame sequence that restores about one texture per
frame never reaches the cap.
Test: graphics/tests/angle_texture_parking.mm --in-use waits for the background repacks before the working set comes
back, samples the footprint after every draw while it is restored and requires 256 MiB to stay available then
(patch 0019: 0 MiB, the footprint 500 MiB over the limit; this patch: ~390 MiB).
Upstream: no (Minato's memory policy).
